t is the week and asked in a survey  like this what are your worker shortage  issues do you have worker shortage  issues and I don't have I don't have my  slide I don't have the slide here for  the whole group but what that slide says  is that thirty-six percent of the  growers said in the in the whole of all  those who responded thirty-six percent  said that they were short of workers in  2013 and this gives you a bit of an idea  of a breakout by region of who said they  were sort of workers and who said that  they had sufficient workers and central  New York and northern New York indicated  that they were most short of workers  then we asked them along these same  lines what was the average number of  additional workers that you would have  hired if they were available for your  farm in 2013  and you can see that in in some of the  smaller farms that started out with two  workers and the very largest farm said  they would have hired almost 11 workers  full-time equivalent wise to work on the  farm if those workers were available  then we went one step further and said  well what do you as you look at your  workforce needs in the future what are  you anticipating and how many employees  do you think that you'll need three  years from now fewer less or stays the  same and seventy-four percent said that  they are we're anticipating with the  needs of their business that they were  going to need more employees than what  they hire today and this has real  implications for immigration reform and  how we wear these workers are going to  come from in the future and how we  source labor in the future and then  finally so the last thing that the  grower got in the cert when he was  filling out he or she was filling out  the survey was a couple of open-ended  questions and here's an opportunity for  us to ask some things about they can  answer the question any way they want  now it gets a little bit you have to  then do this grouping lumping process at  the end but we end up writing out all  the answers on summary sheets and it's  very interesting to look at what growers  say in an open-ended question when they  can answer it any way they like so we  asked them first what were the top three  issues that you think grower  organizations that work on your behalf  should work on in the next five years  labor availability and immigration and  h-2a and efficiency and all those things  were far and away above the  following2 so labor labor availability  was number one not surprising government  regulation is number two we hear that  all the time especially as the farm  grows in size and then marketing  consumer education global markets by  local all those marketing issues were  number three so this is what they said  they wanted organizations that they  belong to to do on their behalf in the  years ahead and this just is to  reinforce the point that we hear this  not only in the Apple industry today but  many many labor intensive industries  that labor workforce issues are our  number one management issue and if we  could solve that life would improve in  terms of managing the farm business now  to meet this what is the question that  was most interested me most of interest  to me when we put this together one of  the things that was on my mind a lot is  okay we all know that immigration reform  has stalled but the farm manager is  dynamic operates a dynamic business and  does not stop just because immigration  reform is stalled they have to keep  running this business because they have  such a large capital investment in many  cases so we asked the survey  participants given the labor pressures  that you've experienced in the last  several years including labor being  short how has or will your business  change and so these are in order of the  response again we regrouped their  open-ended responses and in order of  priority this is what they said orchard  design planting systems and  equipment to be more labor efficient was  the biggest th